Subject: client: keep running after a failed buffer swap

The EGL renderer ended the client on the first failed buffer swap:
egl_render returned the swap's result, and a false result breaks the
render loop. On an NVIDIA host (RTX 4070 SUPER, driver 615.78.08) with
video memory nearly full, the first swap after the first guest frame
failed in 13 of 24 opens of the window. The EGL error was 0x3000
(EGL_SUCCESS). A test build that carried on showed one failed swap each
time, and the next swap succeeded.

A failed swap now ends the client only when every swap has failed for
one second. This does not remove the cause of the failed swap, which
is not known. It stops a short run of failed swaps from closing the
window.

- egl.c: egl_render counts the failed swaps in a row. On a failed swap
  it waits SWAP_FAIL_RETRY_NS (10 ms), calls app_invalidateWindow(true)
  and returns true. The frame did not reach the screen, so the damage
  history no longer matches the buffers. The call makes the next frame a
  full redraw and wakes the render thread for the next try. The wait
  keeps a run of failed swaps to about 100 tries: a try asks the
  compositor for a frame callback (with win:jitRender) and for a
  presentation report (when the compositor has that protocol), and both
  wait for the surface's next commit, which a failed swap may not make.
  When swaps have failed for SWAP_FAIL_LIMIT_NS (one second), egl_render
  returns false as before. The first good swap resets the count.

The caller still treats a frame with a failed swap as shown: it retires
the render queue's pending source change and the cursor repaint one try
early, and it counts the frame's timing. The next try redraws all of it.

The log line for a failed swap keeps its text and gains ", trying
again". Only the first and the last failed swap of a run are logged.
The first good swap after them logs "EGL swap succeeded after N failed
in M ms".

